rounds. We can determine which round each forced index belongs to by integer division. Then for each round, we need to include all forced indices that fall in that round. That makes sense.\n\nSo general solution:\n- N = len(round_scores[0]) (assuming consistent)\n- For each round r:\n    - offset = r * N\n    - forced_in_round = [f - offset for f in forced if offset <= f < offset + N]  # local indices\n    - selected_local = set(forced_in_round)\n    - Then fill with top scores from remaining local indices until target count.\n- Then output as before.\n\nBut careful: forced indices might be out of range? Not likely.\n\nGiven the public fixtures, this will work.\n\nNow, about retained_indices: Should they be sorted? The schema doesn't specify. But to be safe, I'll sort them in ascending order. Also, history sublists should be sorted? I'll sort each round's selected global indices ascending as well. That's deterministic.\n\nAlso, ensure we don't mutate case.\n\nNow, write the Python code.\n\nWe need to return a JSON object with key \"implementation\" that is the source code as a JSON string. So we need to produce a string that is valid Python code, then encode it as JSON string (escaping quotes, newlines, etc.). We'll do that.\n\nThe code should define solve(case). It will import nothing except standard library? Possibly need to use math? Not needed.\n\nImplementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    round_scores = case[\"round_scores\"]\n    round_targets = case[\"round_targets\"]\n    forced = case.get(\"forced\", [])\n    N = len(round_scores[0])  # assuming same length\n    history = []\n    all_selected = []\n    for r, (scores, target) in enumerate(zip(round_scores, round_targets)):\n        offset = r * N\n        forced_local = [f - offset for f in forced if offset <= f < offset + N]\n        selected_local = set(forced_local)\n        # create list of (score, local_index) for indices not forced\n        candidates = [(s, i) for i, s in enumerate(scores) if i not in 